WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.600
What if you could use your nose

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.490
to snorkel, or uproot a small tree,

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.040
or smell water from several miles away?

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.470
Well, elephants wouldn't
be that impressed.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:10.780
They do it on the daily,

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.671
thanks to what's inside their trunk.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.660
If you were to dissect an elephant trunk,

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.740
it would actually look more like

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.410
the inside of your tongue than your nose.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.080
Trunks, tongues, and even octopus arms

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.460
are unique organs called
muscular hydrostats.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.500
That means they're made
almost entirely of muscle,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.810
and an elephant's trunk has a lot of them,

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.490
about 40,000, compared
to around 650 muscles

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.410
in the entire human body.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.980
Normally, muscles depend
on bones and joints

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.750
to move and exert force.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.730
When we pick up a dumbbell, for example,

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.180
our bicep pulls on our forearm bones

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.550
and that causes them to swing up

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.080
around our elbow joint.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.010
But in an elephant trunk,
there are no bones to pull

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.410
and no joints to hinge on.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.560
The muscles take on that role instead.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.670
This makes trunks incredibly flexible

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.660
so they can move in all directions.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.040
And all those muscles mean
they're powerful enough

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.900
to lift hundreds of pounds,

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.140
yet delicate enough to
pick up a tortilla chip

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.930
without even cracking it.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.440
But while trunks may be
structured like a tongue

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.400
and function like an arm,
they are, in fact, a nose,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.773
and an exceptional one.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.100
An animal's sense of smell is linked

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.280
to the number of olfactory
receptor genes it has.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.020
And elephants, well,
they have a lot of them,

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.640
nearly 2,000 - more than
any other animal we know of.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.410
Bloodhounds, for example,
only have about 800

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.840
while humans have even fewer.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.210
In fact, an elephant's nose is so good

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.570
it can actually sniff out bombs.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.680
People have reported
that African elephants

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.260
avoid land mines in Angola

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.400
and in 2015, researchers
put it to the test.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.640
Elephants were presented
with a lineup of buckets

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.760
filled with different
smells, including TNT,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.150
the main ingredient in land mines.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.453
Out of 97 TNT samples,
elephants detected all but one.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.930
Of course, their trunks didn't
evolve as bomb detectors.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.440
They use their nose like we use our eyes,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.060
to find food and water,
to avoid predators,

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.520
and to map out other elephants nearby.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.100
That's like walking into a family reunion

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.520
with your eyes closed and knowing

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.900
exactly where everyone is.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.870
But if you can believe it,
there are even more tricks

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.960
up their, trunks.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.640
When elephants traverse
deep rivers, for example,

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.980
they curve their trunk into a snorkel,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.580
and when bathing, they
can use it as a hose,

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.860
or more like a fire hose.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.720
With one suck, a trunk can pull in

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.920
as much as 10 liters of water.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.340
And the trunk's impressive
abilities haven't gone unnoticed.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.000
In fact, if you stick a mirror
in front of an elephant,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.450
one of their favorite activities

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.147
is to open their mouth and
check out their trunks.

00:02:46.147 --> 00:02:49.170
Let's admit it, if you
had a nose like that,

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.323
you would do the exact same thing.

